{
  "id": "reverse-nodes-in-k-group",
  "questionId": "reverse-nodes-in-k-group",
  "slug": "reverse-nodes-in-k-group",
  "title": "Reverse Nodes in k-Group",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "reversal"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "reversing the whole list is a known trick - the twist here is doing it in bounded chunks and knowing when to stop reversing",
    "before reversing a group, you first need to check whether k nodes actually exist starting from here - what happens if you don't check first and the last group happens to be shorter than k?",
    "if a full group of k exists, reverse just that segment using the standard iterative pointer-reversal technique, keeping track of the node before the group and the node after it",
    "after reversing a group, connect the previous group's tail to the new head of this group (which was its last node before reversal), then recurse or iterate on the remainder starting after this group"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "reversing the final group even when it has fewer than k nodes",
    "losing the connection between consecutive reversed groups by forgetting to relink the previous group's tail",
    "off-by-one errors when checking whether k nodes remain before starting a reversal"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order, along with an integer `k`.\n\n**Reverse the nodes of the list `k` at a time and return the resulting list's values in order.**\n\nIf the number of nodes remaining at any point is not a multiple of `k`, leave that final group of nodes as-is, in their original order.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 5000",
      "-1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000",
      "1 <= k <= nums.length"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "k = 1 (no change, since groups of 1 reversed are identical)",
      "k equal to the full list length (entire list reversed)",
      "list length not a multiple of k, leaving a partial trailing group untouched"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2",
        "output": "[2, 1, 4, 3, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 1, 4, 3, 5]",
        "explanation": "reverse (1,2) and (3,4), the leftover single node 5 stays as-is"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 3",
        "output": "[3, 2, 1, 4, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[3, 2, 1, 4, 5]",
        "explanation": "reverse (1,2,3), the leftover (4,5) has fewer than k nodes so it stays as-is"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], k = 6",
        "output": "[6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]",
        "explanation": "the whole list is exactly one group of size k, so it's fully reversed"
      }
    ],
